**Case 1 — Index access (comparing adjacent elements):**

```java
// LOOP: easy — compare trade[i] with trade[i-1]
for (int i = 1; i < trades.size(); i++) {
    if (trades.get(i).getAmount().compareTo(trades.get(i - 1).getAmount()) > 0) {
        log.info("Trade {} amount increased from previous", trades.get(i).getTradeId());
    }
}

// STREAM: awkward — no natural index access
// Workaround: IntStream.range() but loses readability:
IntStream.range(1, trades.size())
    .filter(i -> trades.get(i).getAmount().compareTo(trades.get(i - 1).getAmount()) > 0)
    .forEach(i -> log.info("Trade {} increased", trades.get(i).getTradeId()));
// Works but less readable, and depends on indexed collection (not all sources support get)
```

**Case 2 — In-place modification:**

```java
// LOOP: modify original list
for (Trade trade : trades) {
    if (trade.getStatus().equals("PENDING")) {
        trade.setStatus("PROCESSING");  // in-place mutation
    }
}

// STREAM: violates side-effect-free principle
// trades.stream().forEach(t -> t.setStatus("PROCESSING"));  // BAD! Side-effect in stream!
// Correct stream approach: create NEW list (not modify in-place):
List<Trade> updated = trades.stream()
    .map(t -> t.withStatus("PROCESSING"))  // immutable transform
    .toList();
// But if you NEED to modify the original objects (JPA dirty checking), loop is cleaner
```

**Case 3 — Checked exceptions inside lambda:**

```java
// LOOP: clean try/catch per element
for (Trade trade : trades) {
    try {
        externalApi.settle(trade);  // throws SettlementException (checked)
    } catch (SettlementException e) {
        log.error("Failed to settle {}: {}", trade.getTradeId(), e.getMessage());
        trade.setStatus("FAILED");
    }
}

// STREAM: checked exceptions DON'T work in lambdas without ugly wrappers
trades.stream().forEach(t -> {
    // externalApi.settle(t);  // COMPILE ERROR! Unhandled checked exception!
    try {
        externalApi.settle(t);
    } catch (SettlementException e) {
        // try/catch inside lambda — ugly, defeats the purpose of stream readability
        throw new RuntimeException(e);
    }
});
```

**Case 4 — Complex break/continue with state:**

```java
// LOOP: accumulate until budget exceeded
BigDecimal budget = new BigDecimal("500000");
BigDecimal spent = BigDecimal.ZERO;
List<Trade> executed = new ArrayList<>();
for (Trade trade : trades) {
    if (spent.add(trade.getAmount()).compareTo(budget) > 0) {
        break;  // STOP — budget would be exceeded
    }
    spent = spent.add(trade.getAmount());
    executed.add(trade);
}

// STREAM: takeWhile works but can't track running total easily
// (takeWhile predicate can't mutate spent without violating side-effect-free rule)
```

**Case 5 — Nested loops with labeled break:**

```java
// LOOP: find first trade pair that matches
outer:
for (Trade buy : buyTrades) {
    for (Trade sell : sellTrades) {
        if (buy.getSymbol().equals(sell.getSymbol())
            && buy.getAmount().equals(sell.getAmount())) {
            log.info("Matched: {} ↔ {}", buy.getTradeId(), sell.getTradeId());
            break outer;  // exit BOTH loops
        }
    }
}

// STREAM: flatMap + findFirst works but less obvious for labeled break
```

**Case 6 — Performance-critical hot paths:**

```java
// In tight loops processing millions of primitives:
// LOOP: no autoboxing, no lambda overhead
int sum = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < amounts.length; i++) {
    sum += amounts[i];  // pure primitive, zero object creation
}

// STREAM: IntStream avoids boxing but still has pipeline overhead
int sum = IntStream.of(amounts).sum();  // slightly slower for primitive arrays
// For 99% of use cases the difference is negligible
// Only matters in hot paths identified by profiling
```

